{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["And he says concerning a brother's wife: <b>niddah</b> — the meaning is that one should keep distant from her. So too they said: 'Your brothers, your haters, from your niddim' [Obadiah 1:7?]. Now among the forbidden relations there is no male niddah except in the case of a brother's wife. And the niddah is forbidden, yet a time may come for her to be permitted; likewise it is possible with a brother's wife, as I shall explain in the pericope 'When brothers dwell together' [Deuteronomy 25:5]. And as for what they said — just as a father's sister is forbidden, so a woman is forbidden to marry her uncle — this is not the way, for the male forbidden relations are the ones that impose [prohibition], and there is none to impose upon them. And if by way of equivalence of reasoning, their deficient opinion would seem to us [to imply that] we should not accept the testimony of two women, and if for valuations they [i.e., women] must be four [witnesses] like two witnesses — therefore we are required to accept it [i.e., the prohibition of a woman marrying her uncle]."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The citation 'your brothers, your haters, from your niddim' does not match any biblical verse exactly; it may be a paraphrase or a mis-citation of Obadiah 1:7, and is translated hyper-literally. The latter half of the comment is textually dense and possibly corrupt in places; it is rendered literally without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:11:29.285007139Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}